Splunk Practical 02: Exploring Raw Events and Fields (B2)

1. Objective & Scope
Objective: Expand a raw event to see how Splunk builds fields, and learn to filter and aggregate on those fields.
Environment: Splunk Enterprise (Windows Local Instance)
Target Index: `main` (BOTS v1 dataset not used; my own laptop's Windows Security logs were ingested instead)
Host: Himanshu
Source: WinEventLog:Security
Time Range: All time

Setup (how the data was ingested):
Settings > Add Data > Monitor > Local Event Logs > selected Security > Host: Himanshu, Index: main > Submit.
Total events indexed: 33,068

2. Practical Execution: Raw Events and Fields

SPL Query 1 (view events)
index=main source="WinEventLog:Security" earliest=0
| head 20

SPL Query 2 (EventCode inventory across all data)
index=main source="WinEventLog:Security" earliest=0
| stats count by EventCode
| sort - count

SPL Query 3 (filter out noise)
index=main source="WinEventLog:Security" earliest=0 NOT EventCode=5379
| head 20

3. SPL Query Explanation
index=main : Sets the search target to the index where the Windows logs were ingested.

source="WinEventLog:Security" : Limits results to Windows Security logs only. Quotes are needed because the name contains a colon. (For local logs this value is the source; in BOTS v1 it was the sourcetype.)

earliest=0 : Starts the time range from the oldest available data, so all data is searched.

| head 20 : Returns only the 20 most recent events. This is a sample, not the full picture.

| stats count by EventCode : Counts events for each unique EventCode and builds a table.

| sort - count : Sorts the count in descending order, highest first.

NOT EventCode=5379 : Removes the noisy 5379 events from the results.

4. Steps Performed
1. Ran Query 1 and expanded an event using the left arrow (>).
2. Reviewed the fields in the Interesting Fields sidebar: Account_Name, Account_Domain, ComputerName, EventCode, Keywords, etc.
3. Clicked the EventCode field to open its popup (top values, count, percentage).
4. Ran Query 2 to get the EventCode count across the whole dataset.

5. Observations (Results)
Total events: 33,068
Distinct EventCodes: 25

Top EventCodes:
5379 (28,252): Credential Manager credentials were read. Normal background activity from Windows/apps and the biggest source of noise.
4799 (1,123): Local group membership was enumerated. Normal system activity.
4624 (855): Successful logon.
4672 (786): Special (admin-level) privileges assigned to a new logon.
5061 (528), 5058 (490), 5059 (309): Cryptographic/key operations. Normal.
4798 (194): A user's local group membership was enumerated.
4688 (156): A new process was created.
4648 (133): Logon attempted using explicit credentials.
4634 (73): Logoff.

One 4624 event was expanded and reviewed:
Account Name (Subject): HIMANSHU$ (machine account; the `$` indicates a computer account)
Account Domain: WORKGROUP (PC is not part of a domain)
Logon Type: 5 (service logon)
New Logon Account: SYSTEM
Elevated Token: Yes
Meaning: This was not a human login, but a normal Windows service startup logon.

6. Key Learnings
1. Splunk breaks every log into fields. The fields you can filter or aggregate on are what make an investigation possible (for example, EventCode=4625 for failed logins).
2. `head 20` returns only the 20 most recent events. On my laptop, 5379 fires every few milliseconds, so the EventCode popup showed only 5379 (100%).
3. To get the full picture, use `stats count by field`.
4. High-count events are usually noise. Remove them with NOT to find rare or interesting events.
5. Common Logon_Type values: 2 = interactive (keyboard), 5 = service, 7 = unlock.

7. Useful EventCodes (quick reference)
4624 = successful logon
4625 = failed logon
4672 = special privileges assigned
4688 = new process created
4634 / 4647 = logoff
